## DormNet
This is a Wi-Fi Monitoring and Accessibility Mapping System for Pisay Dormitories. It visualizes collected performance measurements at different dormitory locations and times and analyzes and displays results through accessibility maps and summaries. The system aims to help improve Wi-Fi distribution and accessibility within dormitories.

## I. Project Title
# DormNet: A Wi-Fi Monitoring and Accessibility Mapping System for Pisay Dormitories

## II. Problem Statement
Reliable Wi-Fi access is important for students living in the Pisay dormitories because many academic activities require internet connectivity, including research, accessing learning platforms, submitting requirements, communicating with teachers and classmates, and completing online activities. However, Wi-Fi performance may vary depending on a student's location within the dormitory. Some areas may experience weaker signal strength, slower speeds, or higher latency than others.
At present, students may know that certain areas have poor connectivity, but there may not be an organized way to collect, compare, and visualize Wi-Fi performance throughout the dormitory. This makes it difficult to identify patterns and determine which areas require attention.
DormNet aims to address this problem by creating a program that allows users to record Wi-Fi performance measurements at different dormitory locations and times. The program will organize the collected information, analyze the measurements, and display the results through tables, summaries, and a Wi-Fi accessibility map. The system can help students and authorized school personnel better understand the distribution and performance of Wi-Fi within the dormitory.

## III. Project Objectives
By the end of the project, the program aims to:
Develop a program that records and organizes Wi-Fi measurements such as signal strength, download speed, upload speed, and latency together with the location and time of each measurement.
Analyze the collected data to identify areas with relatively weak or inconsistent Wi-Fi performance and summarize the results in an easy-to-understand format.
Create a visual representation of Wi-Fi accessibility in the dormitory that can help users identify areas with different levels of connectivity.

## IV. Planned Features
1. Wi-Fi Measurement Input
The user can enter information collected from a Wi-Fi testing tool, including:
Location
Date and time
Signal strength
Download speed
Upload speed
Latency/ping
2. Data Storage
The program will organize the measurements into a structured dataset.
Example:
Location
Time
Signal
Download
Upload
Ping
Room A
6:00 PM
-55 dBm
45 Mbps
18 Mbps
24 ms
Room B
6:00 PM
-72 dBm
19 Mbps
9 Mbps
